<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Issus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">333 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Issus · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-alexander-the-great">Wars of Alexander the Great</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Issus was fought in 333 BCE near the Gulf of Issus in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a>. Alexander won, and Darius fled, leaving his family to be captured. The victory opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus.jpg" alt="Battle of Issus" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Issus_mosaic_-_Museo_Archeologico_Nazionale_-_Naples_2013-05-16_16-25-06_BW.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive military collapse came only with Alexander the Great's campaigns (334–330 BCE), when a Macedonian army of exceptional tactical sophistication defeated Persian forces at the Granicus, <b>Issus</b> and Gaugamela in rapid succession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His conquest of the Achaemenid Persian Empire included victories at the Granicus (334 BCE), <b>Issus</b> (333 BCE) and Gaugamela (331 BCE), the sieges of Tyre and Gaza (332 BCE), and the defeat of Porus at the Hydaspes (326 BCE), demonstrating ingenuity in both pitched battle and siege warfare.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating the Persians at the <b>Battle of Issus</b> in 333 BCE, Alexander the Great marched down the coast.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Odrysians were long allies of Athens and joined the Peloponnesian War on its side; in the 4th century BCE Philip II of Macedon conquered southern Thrace, and Thracian cavalry and javelin men later served Alexander the Great at <b>Issus</b> and Gaugamela.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>After the battle of <b>Issus</b>, the satrap Mazaces surrendered Egypt to Alexander the Great without a fight in 332 BCE, and it passed to the Macedonian Empire and later the Ptolemaic Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
